  "review_text": "<b>Best Panel of the Year</b>\n\n[LINK:1963:FF_14|FF #14|2026-08-06]&nbsp;     bottom of page 16 -- the Thing has had <i>enough</i> of the Sub-Mariner's deep sea fungus (and apparently of Namor's endless chatter)!\n[ASSET: center | ThingHasHadEnough | width=50%]\n[LINK:1963:ASM_3|ASM #3|2026-09-11]&nbsp;     front lit Doctor Octopus\n[ASSET: center | DocOckFrontLit | width=50%]\n[LINK:1963:ASM_4|ASM #4|2026-10-26]&nbsp;     Spider-Man bursts in on the Sandman\n[ASSET: center | SpiderManBurstsIn | width=50%]\n\n<b>Worst Story of the Year</b>\n\n[HANG0] [LINK:1963:HULK_6|HULK #6|2026-07-07] - the ill-fated Metal Master showed us that no matter how bad the <i>previous </i>issues of the Hulk magazine had been, [SPAN: severity=error; text=we hadn't seen <i>nothing</i> yet!]\n[HANG0] [LINK:1963:TOS_41_iron0|TOS #41|2026-08-12] -- the <i>first</i> Dr. Strange!&nbsp; (which I note could also have taken the Spiral Award since we go from villain introduction to cheers from \"power-mad military men\" from all over the world over the course of three story pages!&nbsp; Thank goodness there was a flashlight nearby!&nbsp; <i>Holy cow!</i>\n[HANG0] [LINK:1963:AVEN_2|AVEN #2|2027-01-09] -- the Space Phantom debuts in a tale riddled with so many plot holes to make one's head spin -- <i>yeesh</i>!\n\nFrom the candidates <i>above</i> however, I give the nod to:&nbsp; xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx\n\n\n<b>Biggest Blunder</b>\n\n[LINK:1963:ASM_3|ASM #3|2026-09-11]&nbsp;     Doc Ock calls Spider-Man \"Super-Man\"\n[ASSET: center | DocOckSuperMan | width=50%]\n\n<b>Best Back-Up Story</b>\n[BR] There were actually some pretty good back-up tales to be found buried amidst this year's batch of books.\n\n[HANG0] [LINK:1963:TOS_44_iron0|TOS #44|2026-10-14] -- \"One of Us Must Die\"\n[HANG0] [LINK:1963:TOS_47_iron0|TOS #47|2027-01-03] -- \"Shock!\"\n[HANG0] [LINK:1963:TTA_51_giant0|TTA #51|2027-02-11] -- \"No Place to Turn!\"\n\nBut none of these held a candle to [LINK:1963:TTA_47_ant0|TTA #47|2026-10-23]'s \"Target... Earth!\"&nbsp; However, since that one was primarily best for its laugh-out-loud moment, see Best Belly-Laugh Moment award elsewhere.\n\nFrom the candidates <i>above</i> however, I give the nod to:&nbsp; xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx\n\n<b>Best Belly-Laugh Moment</b>\n\nThere were some funny moments this year:\n\n[LINK:1963:ASM_7|ASM #7|2027-01-21] -- The sight of J. Jonah Jameson arguing with the Vulture, trying to convince him <i>not</i> to rob his payroll, <i>heh</i>.\n\n[DEVNOTE: Target...Earth!&nbsp; (put here for searching purposes)]\n\nThis award actually goes to the <b>backup</b> tale in [LINK:1963:TTA_47_ant0|TTA #47|2026-10-23] called \"Target... Earth!\" (by Stan Lee and Steve Ditko).\n\nVery much in line with one of the 1961's Trope Trifecta, this one detailed Ba-Korr and the warriors of the planet Klang, who are looking for a new world to conquer, and lo and behold they find \"a weak planet named Earth! (<i>Oh no!</i>)&nbsp; \"in a far-off galaxy\" no less!&nbsp; Ba-Korr sends Sagar, his mightiest warrior to scout Earth's defenses... and get <i>this</i> -- if he doesn't report back within 48 hours, the invasion will be called off!\n\nAnd so, with only 48 hours to do his scouting job, Sagar is somehow able to travel to the \"far-off galaxy\" and find that Earth will prove no match for their forces, but he never reports back, so the invasion is called off.&nbsp; Why, you ask?&nbsp; Because of where he parked his ship.&nbsp; When he goes back to his ship to signal to Ba-Korr that the invasion should proceed as planned, he finds his ship gone -- he's trapped!\n\nThe story's final panel that reveals why the ship disappeared became the year's best laugh-out-loud moment.\n[ASSET: center | SlowSinkingQuicksand | width=67%]\n\nHe parked his space ship in a bog of \"slow-sinking quicksand.\"&nbsp; And to add to the humor, he concludes that, \"There is but one thing to do... I must <i>follow</i> my ship to the bottom of this bog -- to remain there for all eternity -- to pay for my fatal error!\"&nbsp; <i>OMG!&nbsp; </i>What a silly story, but one with such an ironic ending that I burst out laughing!&nbsp; <i>Hahahaha!</i>\n\nOnce again, these aliens from \"far-off\" galaxies somehow are able to detect Earth and want to conquer it, even though there must be countless planets in their <i>own</i> galaxies that would be much more convenient!&nbsp; But, perhaps not!&nbsp; I mean, if you're able to travel to Earth <i>and</i> back within a mere 48 hours, despite the distance, perhaps <i>any</i> planet is convenient!\n\n[ASSET: right | CreepyTinkerer | width=12%] However, I also want to give out an Honorable Mention for the face of the Tinkerer in the second story that made up [LINK:1963:ASM_2_asm1|ASM #2|2026-07-31].&nbsp; Steve Ditko strikes just the right look for a character who turns out to be every bit as creepy as this panel shows him to be.\n[BR]\n[BR] (Not only that, but -- you guessed it -- he's also an alien who's come to subjugate Earth!)\n\n<b>Spiral Award</b>\n\n[HANG0] [LINK:1963:JIM_91_thor0|JIM #91|2026-07-25] -- Sandu goes from \"Wow, look, I can levitate things!\" to \"I can rule the world!\" over the course of three pages, all while Loki pulls the strings from up in Asgard.&nbsp; (But <i>hey</i>, it's the Silver Age -- there's no time to <i>waste</i>!)\n\n[ASSET: right | MisterMxyzptlk | width=12%] [HANG0] [LINK:1963:JIM_95_thor0|JIM #95|2026-10-20] -- Professor Zaxton goes \"world's foremost theoretical physicist\" to maniacal would-be ruler of the world in only 8 pages, creating an evil duplicate of Thor in the process, not to mention entire office buildings, planes, and ultimately himself -- logic be damned!&nbsp; I kept waiting for Mister Mxyzptlk to appear -- it seemed right up his alley.&nbsp; <i>Argh!</i>"
}